question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297364cd-4444-5a41-ba36-21b226267ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-11637" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{2}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement $CD=DC={I}_{2}$ is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement $D={C}^{-1}$ is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $DE=ED={I}_{2}.$ Computing $DE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement $DE=ED={I}_{2}$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $CD=DC={I}_{2}$; $D={C}^{-1}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050064 :topic/id #uuid "2a546664-10ba-55b4-ae58-ccb5dd16dca0" :topic/math-academy-id 1483 :topic/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id 