item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73afeb6e-bb5d-5e60-a750-aabc89815638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422254 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409283 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253524} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} :answer-field/id #uuid "c68d0e4a-42ad-5fc6-a993-d34e08e87259" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e075483-5dc5-5bbb-be2d-e2d1164eea57" :question/math-academy-id "q-118870" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$ is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$ only." :task-item/_conte