:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c2d95ae-9363-5355-83b9-696bbda61d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463692} :answer-field/id #uuid "02147d49-bddf-5582-87f2-fa1cfc36d5c7" :answer-field/key "$(-5,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94044061-c912-5dba-951d-29ed12746c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129177" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{x-3}?$ \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[1,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=3.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-5,-1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-5,-1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-5,3)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-5,-1]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980 :topic/id #uuid "d0f342d9-c70c-537f-9e04-f3b0beaf9c01" :topic/math-academy-id 2083 :topic/title "Row Echelon Form"}]}] 